+## Revision Model
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+Each accepted submission becomes one immutable revision of the visit. A revision carries:
+
+| Attribute | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| visit ID | the client-generated UUID of the visit |
+| revision ID | server-assigned, unique per visit, immutable |
+| base revision | the revision the edit was made against; absent for a visit's first submission |
+| payload | the complete submitted visit: plot reference, observation date, observer names, species cover estimates, notes |
+| author ID | the authenticated submitting worker |
+| receipt time | server clock at acceptance |
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+A submission whose base revision is the visit's head (or that starts a new visit) extends the revision chain directly: it becomes the new head, and the visit's current state is that revision's payload.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+A submission whose base revision is not the visit's head is a **competing revision**: it is retained in full, but the head does not move, and the visit is flagged as having unresolved review work. Arrival order never decides between competing revisions — that choice belongs to a coordinator.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+While a visit has unresolved work, its current state stays at the last agreed head. A coordinator resolution is the only operation that moves the head past a fork. Resolution adopts one of the competing revisions (the adopted revision becomes the head and the visit is resolved); every competing revision, adopted or not, remains stored and retrievable as history.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+First submissions create the visit's revision 1 with no base revision. A base revision must reference a revision of the same visit; submissions naming an unknown or foreign base are rejected.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Submission and Retrieval
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Validate the base revision reference as above. The original payload is preserved exactly as submitted, never rewritten into a resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work: a visit response carries its current payload, its head revision ID, and an unresolved flag. When unresolved, the response also lists the open competing revisions so a caller can distinguish "one agreed record" from "two records awaiting a coordinator." Revision-history retrieval returns every revision of a visit with its base revision, so the fork shape is reconstructable from the store alone.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around this envelope: it retains the base revision ID per draft, submits complete payloads, and reads back the unresolved flag and competing-revision list to tell a worker their edit is awaiting review. Review-export sizes its coordinator interface around the unresolved-work representation (comparison view over competing revisions) and its export eligibility rule around the unresolved flag — a visit with unresolved work holds out of the CSV until resolved. Both link back to this node for revision semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for independently edited visits | Whole-visit revisions: every edit is a complete revision naming its base; forks go to coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge: combine disjoint field edits automatically, review only overlaps | The team prefers a larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that could silently combine scientifically related edits (e.g. a species re-identification and its cover estimate) without noticing. Also keeps submission payloads simple (complete visits, no structured diffs), which keeps tablet-sync's offline storage straightforward. |
+| How a fork is resolved | Adopt one competing revision as the new head | Coordinator-authored replacement revision combining edits | A replacement authoring flow can be added later without changing stored history; adopting first keeps the resolution operation minimal. |
+| Current state while a fork is open | Stay at the last agreed head; arrival order never promotes a competitor | Promote first-arriving revision to head, flag the later one | The tenet forbids treating arrival order as authority; staying put makes "awaiting review" unambiguous to every caller. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a coordinator may record a resolution payload that is not one of the competing revisions (a combined record), and how it would be stored and attributed. Decide with review-export's comparison screen design.
+2. Whether a worker may withdraw their own submitted revision before resolution. Not needed until tablet-sync reports a need.
